{"conjecture": "The graph diameter of implicit invariants—untested inter-module constraints that hold only by coincidental alignment of current implementations—expands superlinearly over time because each locally correct, reviewed change adds new conditional dependencies between distant modules without severing existing ones. This expanding diameter increases the cognitive cost of modification because any change must now be checked against a growing set of non-local constraints that are invisible to local tests and reviews. The system becomes harder to change not because any individual module degrades, but because the transitive closure of implicit invariant dependencies grows combinatorially, making the search space of safe modifications shrink exponentially with each accepted change."}
